<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; border: 2px solid #a7d9ed; padding: 15px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; color: #1a5276;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> This Beautiful Day</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's Raining! I Need an Umbrella.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> 
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mediation: Text: Interpret short, simple messages from class texts. Concept: Use simple analogies to explain basic concepts.</p>
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> 
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Mediation: Can interpret simple weather messages using gestures (e.g., points to the sun for "It's sunny."). Can explain weather concepts with simple analogies (e.g., "Rain is like a shower." and mimes rain). Can show understanding of weather words by pointing to visuals (e.g., points to an umbrella for "It's rainy.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> "Weather Charades Review" (5 minutes)</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows weather flashcards (sun, rain, cloud, wind) and performs a simple mime for each (e.g., spreading arms for "sunny", wiggling fingers downwards for "rainy"). Students call out the word.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher then asks: "How do you feel on a sunny day?" (students answer "I feel happy!"). "How do you feel on a rainy day?" (students answer "I feel sad!" or "I feel cold!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks students to gesture their feelings (e.g., smiling for happy, shivering for cold).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> (5 minutes)</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher holds up a picture of "rainy weather" and says: "It's rainy. I need an umbrella. Rain is like a big shower from the sky." (Teacher mimes rain). "When it rains, I get wet!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then holds up a picture of "sunny weather" and says: "It's sunny. I feel happy! The sun shines bright. Sunny is like a big, warm lamp in the sky." (Teacher points to the sun in the picture and smiles).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher emphasizes contractions: "It's sunny," "I'm happy."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> (2 minutes)</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a rainy picture and miming rain) "Am I showing you rain or saying 'sunny'?" (Expected answer: Showing rain.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(After explaining "Rain is like a shower") "Is 'shower' a big rain or a little rain?" (Expected answer: A big rain.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "This Beautiful Day")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated simple dialogue and weather analogy visuals.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher presents a simple dialogue on the board with accompanying visuals (e.g., stick figures, weather icons).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Dialogue example:</b><br>
                        Student A (pointing to a cloud picture): "Look! It's cloudy. How do you feel?"<br>
                        Student B (shrugging): "I'm lazy. Cloudy is like a soft, grey blanket in the sky."<br>
                        Student A (pointing to a windy picture): "Oh! It's windy now! I need my coat."<br>
                        Student B: "Windy means the air blows fast. It's cold!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads the dialogue, pointing to visuals and demonstrating gestures/mimes for clarity (e.g., shivering for cold, making wind sound for windy).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher explicitly points out the analogy: "Cloudy is like a soft, grey blanket." and "Windy means the air blows fast."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing weather and feelings: "I'm happy." "It's raining." Asking and answering about feelings politely: "How do you feel?" "I feel happy." Expressing emotions and needs: Using polite phrases to describe personal experiences (e.g., "I feel happy, thank you." "I need help your umbrella, please.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> (7 minutes)</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "Are the students talking about food or weather?" (Expected: Weather)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (using thumbs up/down):</b> "Student B feels happy when it's cloudy." (Expected: Thumbs down - lazy)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Teacher holds up pictures of a blanket and a windy icon. "Which picture means 'the air blows fast'?" (Students point to windy icon). "Which picture means 'cloudy is like this'?" (Students point to blanket).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Contractions ("I'm," "it's"), WH-questions ("How do you feel?"), Present simple ("I feel happy"). Vocabulary: sun, rain, cloud, wind, umbrella, coat, boots, sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy, happy, sad, bored, excited, lazy, cold. Pronunciation: /s/ in "sunny", /r/ in "raining", blending /r-eɪn/ for "rain". Pragmatic: Describing weather and feelings, asking/answering politely. Sociolinguistic: Expressing emotions and needs politely.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> (15 minutes)</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: "Match the Analogy" (Controlled practice - 5 minutes)</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides worksheets with simple weather pictures on one side (e.g., sun, rain, cloud) and short, mixed-up analogies/descriptions on the other side (e.g., "like a big shower," "like a warm lamp," "like a grey blanket").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students draw lines to match the weather picture to its analogy.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher models the first one: "Sunny is like a warm lamp. (draws line)."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: "Explain the Feeling" (Semi-controlled substitution drill - 5 minutes)</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Teacher provides sentence frames: "It's ______. I feel ______. Because ______."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose a weather flashcard (e.g., "windy") and a feeling (e.g., "cold").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A says: "It's windy. I feel cold." Student B then mediates by explaining: "Because windy means the air blows fast!" (Teacher provides simple explanations on a poster for support).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students switch roles, practicing different weather and feeling combinations.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: "Weather Report and Explain" (Pair interaction/peer check - 5 minutes)</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students stay in pairs. Each student draws a simple weather picture (e.g., sun, rain) and a feeling face on a small whiteboard or paper.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A shows their drawing to Student B and says: "It's [weather]. I'm [feeling]."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B interprets the drawing and statement, then explains one concept or need. For example, if Student A drew "rainy" and "sad," Student B might say: "Oh, it's rainy. You're sad. Rainy means water falls from the sky, like a shower. You need an umbrella!"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A gives feedback: "Yes!" or "No, I'm excited!"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "This Beautiful Day")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Project 2: Weather Role-Play (10 minutes)</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group chooses one weather scenario (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) to role-play.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They will create a short dialogue (3-4 lines) demonstrating the weather, their feelings, and one student will *mediate* by explaining a simple concept or a need related to the weather, or interpreting another student's action.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> Assigns scenarios and circulates to provide support and sentence frames (e.g., "It's ____. I'm ______. ____ is like ______. I need ____, please."). Reminds students to use gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Example Role-Play (Rainy Scenario):</b><br>
                        Student 1 (mimes getting wet, looks sad): "Oh no! It's raining! I'm cold."<br>
                        Student 2 (holds up hand like an umbrella): "Rainy means water falls down! You need an umbrella, please!"<br>
                        Student 3 (shivering): "Yes, I need my coat and boots too. It's very cold!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on mediating the information about the weather, feelings, and needs to the audience through their role-play, using gestures and simple explanations.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment of learning outcomes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Weather Role-Play" performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Weather Role-Play" in front of the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b> (8 minutes)</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can interpret simple weather messages using gestures (e.g., points to sun for "It's sunny.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can explain weather concepts with simple analogies (e.g., "Rain is like a shower.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows understanding of weather words by pointing to visuals/realia (e.g., points to an umbrella).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (weather, feelings, items) and contractions ("It's," "I'm").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronounces /s/ and /r/ clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Expresses emotions and needs politely.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> (2 minutes)</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I use my hands to show the weather?" (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I explain what the weather means to my friends?" (Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Consolidation and forward planning)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b> (1 minute)</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to explain about the weather today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult to explain?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> (2 minutes)</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well for students to mediate (interpret/explain) weather concepts and feelings? The use of gestures and simple analogies seemed very effective for A1.1.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to better explain concepts using analogies? Perhaps more visual aids for analogies or sentence starters for explanations.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "It's Raining! I Need an Umbrella." theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, likely starting with new vocabulary and listening comprehension.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ / ___</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>